+## Verdict on this exact SHA
+
+**YES ONLY AFTER NAMED FIXES — not YES unchanged, and not NO.** I would vote YES on `9af350aa…` **if and only if** fixes **F1–F7** below are incorporated into the balloted text (or recorded, source-linked, byte-verified facts on canonical `main` that make them true). If the bill goes to ballot unchanged, my vote is **NO**, for the reasons in O1–O8: the §3 exceptions are unauditable as drafted, and the §3/§5 language can be read to remove the very byte-verification that makes the §4 "preserved" controls real — while §4's prose would still be cited as if it preserved them.
+
+I withhold a NO-not-fixable verdict because the bill's design is genuinely narrow: it correctly targets only pre-start machinery, adds no paid-spending authority, and its §4 names every substantive safeguard by pointer. The defects are about **whether those promises are enforceable and unambiguous**, not about reversing the Founder's simplification. That is fixable with short clauses that restore no prelaunch gate.
+
+**The single strongest case against the bill:** it can be complied with while an Executive declares the byte-verification that enforces the zero-billed control a "mere formality", declares the per-office ceilings "a mere pre-start cap", admits a training-enabled or region-restricted route under "ordinary parity", and leaves the 48 h fail-closed review and the Spying allowlist as prose — then points at §4 to claim all safeguards were "preserved without change." Nothing in the filed text gives Economy or the Judiciary an independent, source-linked, byte-level veto over those classifications.
